Z||000000||86SE||06-01-2000||08:03 PM||||I took the plugs out a a 84' 4 cyl Fiero one by one to test them.  Only the 2 on the left (drivers side) sparked!!  I couldent get the other 2 to spark.  The plugs are good, the wires are good, new dist cap and rotor and ignition module.<P>Could a week coil keep the other 2 from fireing??  The last to cylinders are the longest distance from the distributor, so mabey there is not enough juice in the coil to make it all the way??||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phaeton||06-01-2000||10:33 PM||||C'mon, they can't all be good.<BR>Pull a switcheroo, put nonsparking plug on sparking wire and sparking plug on nonsparking wire.  Anything change?<BR>The difference between the short and long cables should not matter if they are both within specs.<BR>Did this happen at the same time the new parts were put in? or is this why they were put in?||||reg||12
Z||000002||batboy||06-01-2000||10:41 PM||||If this was a 87 or 88 DIS 2.5, I'd say it was one of the coil packs (2 coils each firing 2 cylinders). Hmmm, don't know, crossed plug wires that are shorting?||||reg||1
Z||000003||86SE||06-01-2000||10:41 PM||||Ok, now I switched it all back to the old parts (coil).  And now all 4 spark, bluish orange spark.  Still, 2 plugs were wet and 2 dry, the 2 dry ones smelled like exhaust, but I could not hear the engine fire.||||reg||8
Z||000004||triker||06-02-2000||10:56 AM||||I'd say it's the coil.  The spark should jump a half inch or more.  It wont jump near as far under compression and that's where most people go wrong in eliminating the coil as the problem.||||reg||1
